§§ `Reading a cell`

-- Seniority beyond 36 reads the last row: the table stops at 36 and prints no
-- further step. NOTES.md § 5 records that no instrument says so in terms, and
-- that this is the encoding's reading of a table that ends.
GIVEN `the table` IS A `a salary table`
      `the seniority` IS A NUMBER
      `the rank` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the combined salary for a full post` `the table` `the seniority` `the rank`
    @nlg the combined salary for a full post at the given seniority and rank
    MEANS
    CONSIDER `the table`
    WHEN `the BA table — rating code 101` THEN
        `the amount in the row at rank` (`the BA table row at seniority` `the seniority`) `the rank`
    WHEN `the MA table — rating code 102` THEN
        `the amount in the row at rank` (`the MA table row at seniority` `the seniority`) `the rank`

§§ `The frontier the table actually separates`

-- The table separates only so many ranks at a given seniority; the rest of
-- the row is the repeat described in the header. This is that staircase, read
-- off the printed tables. It is IDENTICAL in the 1.1.2015, the 1.9.2016, the
-- 1.9.2022 and the corrected agorot printings, which is evidence that it is a
-- designed feature and not an artefact of one typesetting.
GIVEN `the seniority` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the highest rank the table separates at seniority` `the seniority`
    @nlg the highest rank the table separates at the given seniority
    MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the seniority` AT MOST 1  THEN 1
           IF `the seniority` EQUALS 2    THEN 1
           IF `the seniority` EQUALS 3    THEN 2
           IF `the seniority` EQUALS 4    THEN 2
           IF `the seniority` EQUALS 5    THEN 2
           IF `the seniority` EQUALS 6    THEN 3
           IF `the seniority` EQUALS 7    THEN 3
           IF `the seniority` EQUALS 8    THEN 4
           IF `the seniority` EQUALS 9    THEN 4
           IF `the seniority` EQUALS 10   THEN 4
           IF `the seniority` EQUALS 11   THEN 5
           IF `the seniority` EQUALS 12   THEN 5
           IF `the seniority` EQUALS 13   THEN 5
           IF `the seniority` EQUALS 14   THEN 6
           IF `the seniority` EQUALS 15   THEN 6
           IF `the seniority` EQUALS 16   THEN 6
           IF `the seniority` EQUALS 17   THEN 6
           IF `the seniority` EQUALS 18   THEN 7
           IF `the seniority` EQUALS 19   THEN 7
           IF `the seniority` EQUALS 20   THEN 7
           IF `the seniority` EQUALS 21   THEN 7
           IF `the seniority` EQUALS 22   THEN 8
           IF `the seniority` EQUALS 23   THEN 8
           IF `the seniority` EQUALS 24   THEN 8
           IF `the seniority` EQUALS 25   THEN 8
           IF `the seniority` EQUALS 26   THEN 9
           IF `the seniority` EQUALS 27   THEN 9
           IF `the seniority` EQUALS 28   THEN 9
           IF `the seniority` EQUALS 29   THEN 9
           IF `the seniority` EQUALS 30   THEN 9
           IF `the seniority` EQUALS 31   THEN 9
           IF `the seniority` EQUALS 32   THEN 9
           IF `the seniority` EQUALS 33   THEN 9
           IF `the seniority` EQUALS 34   THEN 9
           IF `the seniority` EQUALS 35   THEN 9
           OTHERWISE                          9

-- Equivalently, and this is the form the 14 January 2025 decision itself uses
-- when it describes the grids: rank d is first separated at seniority
-- 1 / 3 / 6 / 8 / 11 / 14 / 18 / 22 / 26.
GIVEN `the rank` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the seniority at which rank is first separated` `the rank`
    @nlg the seniority at which the given rank is first separated
    MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the rank` AT MOST 1 THEN 1
           IF `the rank` EQUALS 2  THEN 3
           IF `the rank` EQUALS 3  THEN 6
           IF `the rank` EQUALS 4  THEN 8
           IF `the rank` EQUALS 5  THEN 11
           IF `the rank` EQUALS 6  THEN 14
           IF `the rank` EQUALS 7  THEN 18
           IF `the rank` EQUALS 8  THEN 22
           OTHERWISE                   26

§§ `The steps the table prints in its own margins`

-- The shekel printing of the table does not only give amounts. Its left
-- margin prints a PERCENTAGE against each seniority row, and its top margin
-- prints one against each rank column. Those percentages are the table's own
-- account of how it was built, and § 35(b)(2) contemplates the table being
-- updated "at the dates and by the rates" set out — so the rates are
-- operative text, not decoration. (The corrected agorot printing carries the
-- grids alone and prints no margins, which is why they are cited to the
-- shekel printing.)
--
-- This encoding therefore carries BOTH: the amounts above, and the steps
-- below, and asserts that the second generates the first.

GIVEN `the seniority` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the seniority step at` `the seniority`
    @nlg the seniority step printed against the given row
    MEANS
    -- Read from the table's left margin: 2% for rows 2 to 7, 1% from row 8.
    -- Row 1 prints no step, being the base.
    BRANCH IF `the seniority` AT MOST 1 THEN 0%
           IF `the seniority` AT MOST 7 THEN 2%
           OTHERWISE                         1%

`the rank step` MEANS 7.5%
    -- Printed once across the top of both 2022 tables. The tables in force
    -- from 1.1.2015 print 8.5% over the three highest columns instead; that
    -- earlier vintage is not encoded here (NOTES.md § 4).

§§ `The table agrees with the steps it prints`

-- Two helpers, so that each assertion below fits the single line a directive
-- must occupy. They add nothing: each is the lookup above with its table
-- already chosen.
GIVEN `the seniority` IS A NUMBER, `the rank` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`BA cell` `the seniority` `the rank`
    MEANS `the combined salary for a full post` `the BA table — rating code 101` `the seniority` `the rank`

GIVEN `the seniority` IS A NUMBER, `the rank` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`MA cell` `the seniority` `the rank`
    MEANS `the combined salary for a full post` `the MA table — rating code 102` `the seniority` `the rank`

-- THE TOLERANCE IS HALF AN AGORA, AND IT IS TIGHT. Applying a printed step to
-- a printed cell lands within 0.005 of the next printed cell — which is
-- exactly the rounding of a figure printed to two decimals, and no more.
-- Measured over the corrected tables: 188 rank steps and 215 seniority steps
-- in each, worst deviation 0.0102 for a single step and 0.0050 for the whole
-- grid generated from its base. The shekel printing needs a tolerance two
-- hundred times larger (1.00) for the same claim, which is itself the
-- evidence that the shekel figures are roundings of these and not a
-- separately-computed table.
GIVEN `the computed amount` IS A NUMBER, `the printed amount` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `agrees to the agora` `the computed amount` `the printed amount` IF
        `the computed amount` MINUS `the printed amount` AT MOST 0.011
    AND `the printed amount` MINUS `the computed amount` AT MOST 0.011

-- The seniority step, down the first rank column: 2% up to row 7, 1% from
-- row 8. Each assertion takes a printed cell, applies the step printed in the
-- margin of the row below it, and meets the cell printed there.
#ASSERT `agrees to the agora` (`BA cell` 1 1 TIMES (1 PLUS `the seniority step at` 2)) (`BA cell` 2 1)
#ASSERT `agrees to the agora` (`BA cell` 6 1 TIMES (1 PLUS `the seniority step at` 7)) (`BA cell` 7 1)
#ASSERT `agrees to the agora` (`BA cell` 7 1 TIMES (1 PLUS `the seniority step at` 8)) (`BA cell` 8 1)
#ASSERT `agrees to the agora` (`BA cell` 20 3 TIMES (1 PLUS `the seniority step at` 21)) (`BA cell` 21 3)
#ASSERT `agrees to the agora` (`BA cell` 35 1 TIMES (1 PLUS `the seniority step at` 36)) (`BA cell` 36 1)
#ASSERT `agrees to the agora` (`MA cell` 1 1 TIMES (1 PLUS `the seniority step at` 2)) (`MA cell` 2 1)
#ASSERT `agrees to the agora` (`MA cell` 7 1 TIMES (1 PLUS `the seniority step at` 8)) (`MA cell` 8 1)
#ASSERT `agrees to the agora` (`MA cell` 26 6 TIMES (1 PLUS `the seniority step at` 27)) (`MA cell` 27 6)
#ASSERT `agrees to the agora` (`MA cell` 35 1 TIMES (1 PLUS `the seniority step at` 36)) (`MA cell` 36 1)

-- The rank step, across a row, within the frontier. At seniority 26 the table
-- separates all nine ranks, so every step across that row is a printed one.
#ASSERT `agrees to the agora` (`BA cell` 26 1 TIMES (1 PLUS `the rank step`)) (`BA cell` 26 2)
#ASSERT `agrees to the agora` (`BA cell` 26 2 TIMES (1 PLUS `the rank step`)) (`BA cell` 26 3)
#ASSERT `agrees to the agora` (`BA cell` 26 5 TIMES (1 PLUS `the rank step`)) (`BA cell` 26 6)
#ASSERT `agrees to the agora` (`BA cell` 26 8 TIMES (1 PLUS `the rank step`)) (`BA cell` 26 9)
#ASSERT `agrees to the agora` (`MA cell` 26 1 TIMES (1 PLUS `the rank step`)) (`MA cell` 26 2)
#ASSERT `agrees to the agora` (`MA cell` 26 8 TIMES (1 PLUS `the rank step`)) (`MA cell` 26 9)

-- And lower down the table, where fewer ranks are separated but the step is
-- the same one.
#ASSERT `agrees to the agora` (`BA cell` 11 1 TIMES (1 PLUS `the rank step`)) (`BA cell` 11 2)
#ASSERT `agrees to the agora` (`BA cell` 11 4 TIMES (1 PLUS `the rank step`)) (`BA cell` 11 5)
#ASSERT `agrees to the agora` (`MA cell` 14 5 TIMES (1 PLUS `the rank step`)) (`MA cell` 14 6)
#ASSERT `agrees to the agora` (`MA cell` 3 1 TIMES (1 PLUS `the rank step`)) (`MA cell` 3 2)

§§ `The merged cells are repeats, not steps`

-- Beyond the frontier the value repeats. This is the observable difference
-- between the two readings NOTES.md § 5 sets out, stated as a test rather
-- than as prose: if these were genuine rank steps they would differ by 7.5%,
-- and they do not differ at all.
#ASSERT `BA cell` 3 2 EQUALS `BA cell` 3 9
#ASSERT `BA cell` 1 1 EQUALS `BA cell` 1 9
#ASSERT `MA cell` 6 3 EQUALS `MA cell` 6 9

-- And the frontier is where the repeats begin.
#ASSERT `the highest rank the table separates at seniority` 1  EQUALS 1
#ASSERT `the highest rank the table separates at seniority` 3  EQUALS 2
#ASSERT `the highest rank the table separates at seniority` 6  EQUALS 3
#ASSERT `the highest rank the table separates at seniority` 11 EQUALS 5
#ASSERT `the highest rank the table separates at seniority` 18 EQUALS 7
#ASSERT `the highest rank the table separates at seniority` 26 EQUALS 9
#ASSERT `the highest rank the table separates at seniority` 36 EQUALS 9

-- The two statements of the frontier are the same staircase read from its two
-- ends, so they must agree at every rank.
#ASSERT `the seniority at which rank is first separated` 1 EQUALS 1
#ASSERT `the seniority at which rank is first separated` 2 EQUALS 3
#ASSERT `the seniority at which rank is first separated` 3 EQUALS 6
#ASSERT `the seniority at which rank is first separated` 4 EQUALS 8
#ASSERT `the seniority at which rank is first separated` 5 EQUALS 11
#ASSERT `the seniority at which rank is first separated` 6 EQUALS 14
#ASSERT `the seniority at which rank is first separated` 7 EQUALS 18
#ASSERT `the seniority at which rank is first separated` 8 EQUALS 22
#ASSERT `the seniority at which rank is first separated` 9 EQUALS 26

§§ `Spot checks against the printed page`

-- The four corners and the running examples' own cells, so that a reader can
-- open the scan and check this encoding without reading any L4.
#ASSERT `BA cell` 1  1 EQUALS 6_300
#ASSERT `BA cell` 36 9 EQUALS 16_886.09
#ASSERT `MA cell` 1  1 EQUALS 6_615
#ASSERT `MA cell` 36 9 EQUALS 17_730.40
#ASSERT ROUND (`BA cell` 11 4) EQUALS 9_172
#ASSERT ROUND (`MA cell` 30 8) EQUALS 15_538
#ASSERT ROUND (`MA cell` 6  3) EQUALS 8_440

-- Seniority past the end of the table reads the last row, and seniority
-- before its start reads the first.
#ASSERT `BA cell` 40 9 EQUALS `BA cell` 36 9
#ASSERT `BA cell` 0  1 EQUALS `BA cell` 1  1

